    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter May 22 with a balanced offensive profile, headlined by **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (81.08) and **Yordan Alvarez** (80.06), our primary targets for production today. **Pete Crow-Armstrong** benefits significantly from a favorable matchup against Arrighetti, where a projected 90% stolen base opportunity window\u2014driven by the opponent\u2019s slow delivery\u2014offsets minor wind concerns. Similarly, **Yordan Alvarez** remains a locked-in core play, as his elite xwOBA of 0.421 provides a high floor against Taillon despite the wind blowing in. We are also capitalizing on high-ceiling leverage with **Junior Caminero** (69.34), who secures a strong score despite the daunting name of Gerrit Cole, as his high-leverage spot in the batting order and favorable park factors maintain his efficiency.\n\nTactical adjustments highlight the scarcity of stolen bases in this league. **Colt Keith** (63.95) and **Trea Turner** (66.48) represent our most consistent engine for R and SB, both benefitting from lead-off assignments. **Casey Schmitt** (57.89) is a nuanced play; while his raw projections are tempered, his \"Elite Hold\" status and favorable wind conditions marginally boost his viability. Regarding bench decisions, **Caleb Durbin** (44.96) was the most difficult exclusion. While his projected speed metrics are notable, the optimizer prioritized the higher platoon-adjusted floor of **Gary Sanchez** and **Christian Yelich**, as **Caleb Durbin** is marginalized by a bottom-of-the-order projection and a difficult matchup against Prielipp. \n\nRisks for today center on the uncertainty of several lineup postings; **Dillon Dingler**, **Colt Keith**, **Casey Schmitt**, **Kazuma Okamoto**, **Christian Yelich**, **Carson Benge**, and **Junior Caminero** are all currently relying on assumed batting positions. Should these lineups shift downward in the order, their efficiency scores\u2014particularly those of **Dillon Dingler** and **Kazuma Okamoto**\u2014could drop into the marginal range. Managers should monitor official reports, particularly for **Christian Yelich**, whose reliance on favorable platoon dynamics against Wrobleski makes him vulnerable to defensive substitutions or pinch-hitting if the game state shifts late. **Zac Veen** remains sidelined in Albuquerque, serving as a reminder to ensure all active roster spots remain optimized for immediate production.",
